Z||000000||SKIDMARK||10-19-2001||12:34 AM||||Did anyone see pictures of the new VW/Lambo? There was an article in the Auto section of the Cleveland Plain Dealer. Sure looks like a Fiero rebody to me!<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||6
Z||000001||Cheever3000||10-19-2001||12:44 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.cleveland.com/autos/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cleveland.com/autos/</A> <P><B>I doubt the link will work for more than a few days, so for those who attempt it later, I'll post the article:</B><P>The first VW Lamborghini <BR>10/18/01<BR>Angus MacKenzie<BR>Special to The Plain Dealer<P>Frankfurt, Germany <P>- The first new Lamborghini in 11 years had a rough ride before it even turned a wheel in public. <P>The launch of the 205-mph Murcielago was delayed more than 18 months at the personal insistence of VW Group Chairman Ferdinand Piech, whose company acquired Lamborghini in 1998 and put it under the direct control of Audi management. <P>Lamborghini's long-awaited replacement for the outrageous Diablo bears the same code name, L147. It has completely different styling and a host of new technical features. <P>Insiders won't confirm that Piech effectively sent L147 back to the drawing board, but suppliers hint that the Murcielago has been a rush program, which lends credence to the idea of a forced redesign. <P>Like previous Lamborghini names, the Murcielago's name is derived from Spanish bullfighting lore; in this case a bull spared in a famous fight in Cordoba in 1879. <P><BR>VW designer at work <P>The Murcielago's low, swooping profile is the work of VW Group designer Luc Donckerwolcke, whose previous credits include the Audi A4 wagon. It's a clean, professional piece of design, but it lacks the drama of previous Lamborghinis, such as the extravagant Diablo, the wedge-shaped Countach and the voluptuous Miura. <P>It's only in the context of its predecessors that the Murcielago seems restrained, however. It's faster, more evocative and more expensive than Ferrari's top-of-the-range, V-12 powered 550 Maranello coupe, and it makes a Porsche 911 Turbo look pedestrian. <P>Only Aston Martin's new 196-mph Vanquish comes close; it has the right badge, the right number of cylinders, and a beautiful body. But it doesn't have the raw, visceral, testosterone-charged presence of its Italian rival. <P>A lack of jewelry in the detailing - particularly around the head- and tail-lights and in the interior - gives the Murcielago a curiously down-market feel up close. <P><BR>How it moves <P>On the move, though, speed-sensitive active air intakes, which automatically pivot 20 degrees to increase cooling capacity, and a three-position active rear spoiler provide a touch of visual theatre. <P>Most of the Murcielago's body is lightweight carbon-fiber; only the scissor doors and roof are steel. The chassis is made of high-strength steel tube bonded to carbon fiber/honeycomb structural elements using adhesives and rivets. <P>The wheelbase has been extended more than half an inch compared with the Diablo, all of it forward of the driver's hip-point to improve room around the pedals. <P>Suspension is race car-style double wishbone all round, with coil-over electronically controlled shocks front and rear. <P>The giant vented and cross-drilled disc brakes feature anti-lock modulation. The 18-inch-diameter alloy wheels are shod with Pirelli P Zero tires 245/35 up front and 335/30 at the rear. <P><BR>Improved engine <P>The Murcielago's 6.2-liter V-12 is improved over the Diablo engine. It now has a dry sump, which allows it to be mounted two inches lower in the car to improve handling, plus variable valve timing and a variable-geometry intake system. <P>According to Lamborghini, peak power is 580 horsepower at 7,500 revolutions per minute (rpm) while the maximum 480 foot-pounds of torque is produced at 5,400 rpm. <P>Lamborghini engineering director Massimo Ceccarani says the variable induction systems enable the V-12 to produce 368 foot-pounds or more of torque from just 2,000 rpm, which should give it an impressive throttle response. <P>The Murcielago uses an upgraded version of Lamborghini's viscous-coupled four-wheel-drive system and a new Lamborghini-designed six-speed manual transmission. <P>Although slightly heavier than the Diablo VT it replaces, Ceccarani says the Murcielago will sprint from zero to 62 mph in just 3.8 seconds. <P>Lamborghini plans to build just 300 Murcielagos a year the V-12 engine is hand assembled at the rate of just 2.5 units a day. <P>The first Murcielagos could arrive in the United States late this fall with prices expected to be about $273,000. <P>But there is more ahead for Lamborghini. <P>Managing director of Automobili Lamborghini, Giuseppe Greco, has disclosed that VW is investing $118 million in the company over five years. <P>About 6 percent of that has already been spent on upgrading the Lamborghini factory at Sant'Agata Bolgnese, near Modena, Italy; the rest has been committed to developing a smaller, less expensive Lamborghini designed to compete directly with Ferrari's F360 and Porsche's 911. <P>That car, due 2003, will increase Lamborghini's total output to about 1,500 cars a year. <P>MacKenzie is the former editor of Wheels magazine in Australia. He is now a free-lance writer based in England. <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
